{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And Jethro, Moses' father-in-law, took a burnt-offering and sacrifices for Elohim.</b> All the commentators wonder why, in the case of all the offerings, there is no mention of either El or Elohim, but only the Ineffable Name (השם המיוחד), whereas here 'for Elohim' is mentioned. And I say to resolve this according to what we wrote above — that all the good things that HaShem did for Moses and for Israel were in the name Elohim, for the righteous transform judgment into mercy; whereas all the evils that HaShem did to Pharaoh and to Egypt were in the Ineffable Name, for the wicked transform mercy into judgment. Therefore Jethro sacrificed to Elohim on account of all the good, and he rejoiced with a glad and good heart and gave thanks to His name, blessed be He, for all the good things that He had done for them. But he did not rejoice over the downfall of the Egyptians, which was wrought in the Ineffable Name; therefore he did not sacrifice to the Ineffable Name, for by this he would have shown that he was grieved over the destruction of Egypt. And for this reason Scripture employs specifically the expression 'וַיִּחַדְ' — for it carries in its meaning both the sense of joy (שִׂמְחָה) and the sense of sharp pangs (חִדּוּדִין): over the good things that befell Israel he rejoiced, and over the destruction of Egypt his flesh was stung with sharp pangs."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:07.054302354Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}